          "id": "doc-eef64596d10c::sentence-18",
          "documentId": "doc-eef64596d10c",
          "text": "The 1896 discovery of radioactivity by Henri Becquerel inspired Marie and Pierre Curie to further investigate this phenomenon. ",
          "start": 1038,
          "end": 1165,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-eef64596d10c::sentence-17",
              "documentId": "doc-eef64596d10c",
              "text": "Work\n",
              "start": 1033,
              "end": 1038,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-eef64596d10c::sentence-19",
              "documentId": "doc-eef64596d10c",
              "text": "They examined many substances and minerals for signs of radioactivity. ",
              "start": 1165,
              "end": 1236,
              "direction": "NEXT"
            }
          ],
          "context": "Work The 1896 discovery of radioactivity by Henri Becquerel inspired Marie and Pierre Curie to further investigate this phenomenon. They examined many substances and minerals for signs of radioactivity."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-a0826bb5a83d::sentence-19",
          "documentId": "doc-a0826bb5a83d",
          "text": "After Marie Skłodowska Curie and Pierre Curie first discovered the radioactive elements polonium and radium, Marie continued to investigate their properties. ",
          "start": 1167,
          "end": 1325,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "After Marie Skłodowska Curie and Pierre Curie first discovered the radioactive elements polonium and radium, Marie continued to investigate their properties."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-eef64596d10c::sentence-14",
          "documentId": "doc-eef64596d10c",
          "text": "Marie was widowed in 1906, but continued the couple's work and went on to become the first person ever to be awarded two Nobel Prizes. ",
          "start": 720,
          "end": 855,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "Marie was widowed in 1906, but continued the couple's work and went on to become the first person ever to be awarded two Nobel Prizes."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-a0826bb5a83d::sentence-15",
          "documentId": "doc-a0826bb5a83d",
          "text": "Marie was widowed in 1906, but continued the couple's work and went on to become the first person ever to be awarded two Nobel Prizes. ",
          "start": 849,
          "end": 984,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "Marie was widowed in 1906, but continued the couple's work and went on to become the first person ever to be awarded two Nobel Prizes."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-eef64596d10c::sentence-12",
          "documentId": "doc-eef64596d10c",
          "text": "She moved to Paris to continue her studies and there met Pierre Curie, who became both her husband and colleague in the field of radioactivity. ",
          "start": 519,
          "end": 663,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-eef64596d10c::sentence-11",
              "documentId": "doc-eef64596d10c",
              "text": "Marie Skłodowska was born in Warsaw, Poland, to a family of teachers who believed strongly in education. ",
              "start": 414,
              "end": 519,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            }
          ],
          "context": "Marie Skłodowska was born in Warsaw, Poland, to a family of teachers who believed strongly in education. She moved to Paris to continue her studies and there met Pierre Curie, who became both her husband and colleague in the field of radioactivity."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-a0826bb5a83d::sentence-13",
          "documentId": "doc-a0826bb5a83d",
          "text": "She moved to Paris to continue her studies and there met Pierre Curie, who became both her husband and colleague in the field of radioactivity. ",
